{
  // STRICT-NULL STAGE 1 — packages/desktop/src/common only.
  //
  // The root config stays untouched on purpose. Flipping `strictNullChecks`
  // there surfaces 297 errors at once, which is not a thing anyone reviews; it
  // is a thing people suppress. This is the first fenced step, and the fence is
  // the `include` below.
  //
  // WHY common FIRST, and not the directory with the most errors. The authority
  // and config cores live here. A null error in `eveAuthorityCore` or
  // `eveAuthorityStoreCore` is not a typing detail — it decides whether a seal
  // is open, whether an amount is a ceiling, whether a grant is trusted. The
  // rule for this directory is therefore: no `?? default` where a decision about
  // authority, money or permission is made, because a default silences the
  // compiler and turns the decision fail-OPEN. Narrow type corrections are right
  // only where the value is genuinely interop noise.
  //
  // THE GATE COMMAND, and why it is not a bare `tsc`:
  //
  //   bunx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.strictnull.common.json 2>&1 \
  //     | grep "error TS" \
  //     | grep -vE 'eveMultimodalGatewayCore|evePdfIntelligenceCore|licenseWireAtRest' \
  //     | wc -l          # must print 0
  //
  // Those three names are a CAP, and it is written into the command so it cannot
  // be missed at the point of use. They account for SIX errors, none of which is
  // a strictNullChecks error: all six are already part of the root baseline (6 of
  // its 19) and all six are one shape — a `Set<string>` assigned to a
  // `readonly T[]`, then `.has()` called on it.
  //
  //   eveMultimodalGatewayCore.ts:395 TS2740, :408 TS2339
  //   evePdfIntelligenceCore.ts:109   TS2740, :122 TS2339
  //   licenseWireAtRest.ts:92         TS2740, :103 TS2339
  //
  // That is a refactor in flight in the working tree. All three files are
  // uncommitted work belonging to someone else and were off limits for this
  // stage, so the errors are excluded from the VERDICT, never from the OUTPUT —
  // running the plain `tsc` line above still prints all six, every time.
  //
  // A `"exclude"` entry was tried first and is deliberately absent: it silenced
  // only `licenseWireAtRest`, because the other two are pulled in transitively by
  // files the `include` does cover. A fence that works on one of three files is
  // worse than none — it reads as complete.
  //
  // Nothing null-related hides behind the cap: with all three files in scope, tsc
  // reports exactly those six and nothing else, so none of them carries a
  // strictNullChecks error today.
  //
  // THE CAP IS PINNED, so it cannot quietly grow. A `grep -v` alone would swallow
  // a genuinely NEW error appearing in one of those three files, and the gate
  // would stay green while coverage silently shrank. Second half of the gate:
  //
  //   bunx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.strictnull.common.json 2>&1 \
  //     | grep "error TS" \
  //     | grep -E 'eveMultimodalGatewayCore|evePdfIntelligenceCore|licenseWireAtRest' \
  //     | grep -cE 'TS2740|TS2339'      # must print exactly 6
  //
  // Measured: 6. Both conditions have to hold — 0 outside the cap AND exactly 6
  // inside it. The error-code filter is what makes the number mean something: a
  // new error of a different kind in a capped file lowers the count below 6 even
  // if it displaced one, so it cannot hide behind a coincidental total.
  //
  // RETIREMENT CONDITION: delete both `grep`s from the command as soon as the
  // Set/array refactor lands and the root baseline drops below 19. The gate then
  // covers all of `common` with no exception at all.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "strictNullChecks": true
  },
  "include": ["packages/desktop/src/common/**/*"]
}
